\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A law on coherent residue sequences}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Fix an integer #{p\ge 2} and real numbers #{\lambda>0} and
#{m>0}. Let #{Q_k}, #{\nu_k}, #{T_k} and #{M_k} be the
precision, law, rescaled class mean and coordinate formula of
the \vocabk{fixed-ratio precision}{fdip-00BY}. There is a
unique probability measure on the sequences
#{(x_k)_{k\ge 0}} satisfying #{x_{k-1}=T_kx_k} for every
#{k\ge 1}, such that #{x_k} has law #{\nu_k}.}

\p{For every such sequence, every #{k\ge 1} and every residue
#{r} modulo #{p^{k-1}},
##{\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}M_k(r+tp^{k-1})=M_{k-1}(r).}
When #{p} is prime, #{M_k(r)} is the ball assignment of the
ball of #{p}-adic integers congruent to #{r} modulo #{p^k},
and the displayed sum is finite additivity for the #{p}
finer balls inside a coarser ball. In that case, if a real
function on the #{p}-adic integers is constant on residue
classes modulo #{p^k} for some #{k}, the sum
#{\sum_r f(r)M_k(r)} is independent of #{k} and linear in
#{f}.}

\proof{
\p{Each coordinate space is a finite product of copies of the
Borel line, so it is second-countable and completely
pseudo-metrizable, and each #{\nu_k} is a probability
measure. For #{\ell<k} the map from depth #{k} to depth
#{\ell} applies #{T_k}, then #{T_{k-1}}, and so on through
#{T_{\ell+1}}. \ref{fdip-00C2} makes each
consecutive pushforward consistent, so the compositions are
consistent. A finite set of coordinates, taken at several
depths, is a function of the coordinates at the finest depth
in the set.}
\p{Mathlib revision \code{727d8bbe6044} supplies the extension
for that class of spaces.
\leanref{MeasureTheory.isProjectiveLimit_projectiveLimit}
constructs a projective limit,
\leanref{MeasureTheory.isProbabilityMeasure_projectiveLimit}
makes it a probability measure, and
\leanref{MeasureTheory.IsProjectiveLimit.unique} gives
uniqueness among finite measures with those projections.
The declarations take the finite-dimensional laws as data.
The Gaussian laws and their covariances are the content of
the [fixed-ratio precision](fdip-00BY) together with
\ref{fdip-000B} and \ref{fdip-00C2}. The projections are
probability measures, so a finite measure with those
projections is a probability measure.}
\p{Set #{\alpha=\sqrt{\frac{\lambda}{p}}}. The definition of
#{T_k} gives
##{(T_kx)(r)=\alpha\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}x(r+tp^{k-1}),}
because #{\frac{\sqrt{p\lambda}}{p}=\alpha}. On a coherent
sequence, #{x_{k-1}=T_kx_k}, and
#{M_k(r)=\alpha^k x_k(r)}, so
##{\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}M_k(r+tp^{k-1})
=\alpha^{k-1}(T_kx_k)(r)
=\alpha^{k-1}x_{k-1}(r)=M_{k-1}(r).}
The identity holds at every coherent sequence.}
\p{Suppose #{p} is prime and #{f} is constant on residue
classes modulo #{p^k}. The sum
#{\Lambda_k(f)=\sum_r f(r)M_k(r)} is linear in #{f}. If #{f}
is also constant on classes modulo #{p^{k-1}}, then #{f}
takes the same value at #{r+tp^{k-1}} as at #{r}, and the
preceding identity gives #{\Lambda_k(f)=\Lambda_{k-1}(f)}.
The same comparison applies to each coarser power of #{p},
so the sum is independent of #{k}.}
}
